public with sharing class ContactDomainService {

    public static Map<String, List<Contact>> groupByEmailDomain(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        Map<String, List<Contact>> result = new Map<String, List<Contact>>();

        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Email, AccountId
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
            AND Email != null
            WITH USER_MODE
        ];

        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (String.isBlank(c.Email)) {
                continue;
            }
            String email = c.Email.trim();
            Integer atIndex = email.lastIndexOf('@');
            if (atIndex < 0 || atIndex == email.length() - 1) {
                continue;
            }
            String domain = email.substring(atIndex + 1).trim().toLowerCase();
            if (String.isBlank(domain)) {
                continue;
            }
            List<Contact> bucket = result.get(domain);
            if (bucket == null) {
                bucket = new List<Contact>();
                result.put(domain, bucket);
            }
            bucket.add(c);
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Notes on the design:

- Apex custom exceptions must end in `Exception` and extend `Exception`. The base class is declared `virtual` so the two subclasses can extend it.
- The subclasses are also declared `virtual`, which is optional but harmless. I left them as plain public classes.
- Exceptions built with `new X('msg')` get their message from the constructor. A custom exception class gets the standard `(String)` constructor automatically, and that carries over to subclasses of a virtual base.
- Regex: `NET\s*(\d{1,3})` matched against the trimmed, upper-cased input. `String.trim()` removes whitespace, and `toUpperCase()` handles case. I use `Pattern` and `Matcher` and require a full match. `NET 0` returns 0, which is fine as it is 1 to 3 digits and not above 180. `NET 000` also gives 0.
- `DUE ON RECEIPT` is matched after trimming and upper-casing. I use `\s+` between the words so that multiple spaces are tolerated. The spec says only "DUE ON RECEIPT", so this is a lenient reading that still fits it.
- The original input is used in the message. String concatenation with a null `terms` yields `"null"`, so that case needs no special handling.
- `dueDate` with a null `invoiceDate` would throw a NullPointerException. I compute the net days first so that terms errors take precedence, and I leave a null date alone because the spec does not define that case.

