The right fit is the Composite API (`/composite`) with `allOrNone` set to false, and the units of work are isolated with separate requests... but that has a catch. In `/composite`, `allOrNone=true` makes the whole call atomic, which would break the isolation between orders. In `/composite/graph`, each graph is its own atomic unit: if one graph fails, only that graph is rolled back and the other graphs are still committed. That matches "one unit of work per order, identified by the order number."

Graph API details:
- Endpoint: `POST /services/data/v59.0/composite/graph`
- Body: `{"graphs":[{"graphId":"SO-1001","compositeRequest":[...]}, ...]}`
- Within a graph, sub-requests use `url`, `method`, `referenceId`, and `body`.
- The upsert of an Account by external ID is `PATCH /services/data/v59.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-1001`, with the body omitting the external ID field itself (it is in the URL).
- The opportunity references the account's upsert result with `@{acct_1001.id}`. Upsert returns an id in both the create (201) and update (204) cases. A 204 on update returns no body, so the reference would not resolve for existing accounts. The Composite Graph docs, however, state that upsert results are referenceable, and the middleware instruction explicitly says to link through the reference to the account's upsert result, so I use `@{acct_1001.id}`.

The graph API allows only the POST, PATCH, PUT and DELETE methods, and PATCH is fine for upserts.

