The key design decision is where to call the service. Opportunity inserts need the record Ids, which only exist after insert, so the call goes in `onAfterInsert` and `onAfterUpdate`, not the before events.

For updates, `onAfterUpdate(Map<Id, SObject> existingRecords)` receives the old records, so I compare each new stage with its old stage. Because `onAfterInsert` and `onAfterUpdate` each fire once per trigger invocation, there is at most one service call per invocation. I guard against an empty set so no call is made when nothing qualifies.

Note that `fflib_SObjectDomain` runs `onAfterUpdate` once per invocation, but a workflow field update can re-fire the trigger. In that case the old stage is already Closed Won, so it is not counted again, which matches the requirement.

The trigger uses `fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler(Opportunities.class)` for all seven events: before insert/update/delete and after insert/update/delete/undelete.

