```apex
public class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {

    private static final String SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'CSAT survey: ';

    private final Set<Id> caseIds;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Set<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = caseIds == null ? new Set<Id>() : new Set<Id>(caseIds);
    }

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this(new Set<Id>{ caseId });
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Case> cases = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :caseIds
            AND IsClosed = true
            AND ContactId != null
        ];
        if (cases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Idempotency: find cases that already have a survey task
        Set<String> existingKeys = new Set<String>();
        Set<String> subjects = new Set<String>();
        for (Case c : cases) {
            subjects.add(SUBJECT_PREFIX + c.CaseNumber);
        }
        for (Task t : [
            SELECT WhatId, Subject
            FROM Task
            WHERE WhatId IN :cases AND Subject IN :subjects
        ]) {
            existingKeys.add(t.WhatId + '|' + t.Subject);
        }

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(2);
        for (Case c : cases) {
            String subject = SUBJECT_PREFIX + c.CaseNumber;
            if (existingKeys.contains(c.Id + '|' + subject)) {
                continue;
            }
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId = c.Id,
                WhoId = c.ContactId,
                Subject = subject,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status = 'Not Started'
            ));
        }

        if (tasks.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Partial success: one failing record doesn't block the others
        Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.insert(tasks, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'CaseSurveyJob failed for case ' + tasks[i].WhatId + ': ' + err.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Putting `Messaging.sendEmail` inside the loop is a bad idea. Salesforce limits you to **10 `sendEmail` invocations per transaction** (the governor limit is on calls, not on emails). With up to 200 opportunities, a call per opportunity would throw `System.LimitException: Too many Email Invocations: 11`. That exception can't be caught, so the whole job would fail after 10 emails.

Instead, build all the messages in the loop and make one `Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false)` call after it. With `allOrNothing=false`, a failure on one email doesn't block the others, which gives you the isolation you wanted. The returned `SendEmailResult` list lets you count the successes. A single call can contain up to 5,000 single emails to internal users by Id, so 200 is fine.

Other points:
- Emails sent with `setTargetObjectId` to a User and `setSaveAsActivity(false)` do not count against the external daily email limit, as you noted.
- The owner could be a queue (a Group Id) rather than a user. Opportunity owners are always users, so this is not a concern.
- Inactive owners can cause failures, so I filter on `Owner.IsActive`. Those are skipped and not counted as sent.
- The query uses `WITH USER_MODE` to respect the running user's access. If the scheduled job runs as a user without access, change it to `SYSTEM_MODE` deliberately.
- Close date is formatted with `format()`, which gives a readable string.
- Email deliverability may be disabled in sandboxes, which would cause failures that show up in the results and are not counted as sent.

