The API for skipping a single action is `MetadataTriggerHandler.bypass(String actionName)`, which takes the Apex class name. `TriggerBase.bypass` would skip every action on the whole sObject, which would also skip phone formatting.

The bypass has to be cleared in a `finally` block so it is removed even if the insert throws. A DML exception from a non-partial insert would otherwise leave the bypass active for later code in the same transaction.

One more consideration: if the bypass was already active when the method was called, clearing it would change the caller's state. So the method checks `isBypassed` first and clears only if it set the bypass itself.

I use the `String` overload with the class name, which matches the registered action. The `System.Type` overload (`TA_Contact_CreateWelcomeTask.class`) would also work, but it adds a compile-time dependency on that class. The string form is safe either way.

If the list is null or empty, the method returns early, avoiding an unnecessary DML statement.

